# SDD Tool Landscape

Spec: SDDToolComparison
Purpose:
  Comparison of major Spec-Driven Development tools and frameworks,
  based on industry research. Used for tool evaluation and selection
  decisions (DEC-XXX).

Owns: references/sdd/tool-comparison.sdd

Tools:

  GitHub Spec Kit:
    type: open-source CLI
    stars: 93,000+
    workflow: Specify → Plan → Tasks → Implement
    features:
      - constitution.md (project-wide immutable rules)
      - works with 30+ AI agents
      - version-controlled specs
    limitations:
      - specs are static (no auto-update during implementation)
      - substantial time overhead (1-3+ hours per feature)
      - no multi-agent orchestration
    best_for: agent-agnostic default, many stacks, portability
    pricing: Free (MIT license)

  AWS Kiro:
    type: agentic IDE (VS Code fork)
    workflow: Requirements (EARS) → Design → Tasks
    features:
      - EARS notation with formal requirements analysis (SMT solvers)
      - agent hooks (automated guardrails)
      - three-document system: requirements.md, design.md, tasks.md
      - auto router: Claude Sonnet, Qwen, DeepSeek, GLM, MiniMax
    limitations:
      - specs are static
      - ecosystem lock-in (Bedrock, .kiro/)
      - metered credits with markup
    best_for: AWS-native teams, greenfield projects
    pricing: Free (50 credits/mo), Pro $20-200/month

  BMAD-METHOD:
    type: open-source framework
    stars: 48,400+
    workflow: multi-agent role-play (Mary, Preston, Winston, Sally, Devon, Quinn)
    features:
      - 21+ specialized agents for full SDLC
      - file-based handoffs with traceable chain
      - BMad Core / BMad Method / BMad Builder layers
      - scale-adaptive intelligence
    limitations:
      - steep learning curve (2+ months)
      - high token costs
      - static specs with file-based handoffs
    best_for: complex builds, full paper trail, compliance
    pricing: Free (MIT license)

  OpenSpec:
    type: open-source framework
    stars: 52,100+
    workflow: proposal → apply → archive
    features:
      - brownfield-first with delta markers (ADDED/MODIFIED/REMOVED)
      - Given/When/Then scenarios
      - strict approval gates
      - openspec validate --strict
    limitations:
      - specs do not update during implementation (static)
      - no multi-agent orchestration
      - manual spec drift management
    best_for: brownfield iteration, change accountability
    pricing: Free (MIT license)

  Cursor with .cursor/rules:
    type: IDE with rules system
    workflow: Plan Mode + AGENTS.md
    features:
      - Plan Mode (read-only planning before implementation)
      - .mdc rule files with YAML frontmatter
      - glob-based rule scoping
    limitations:
      - pseudo-specs (no automated validation)
      - rule activation can be inconsistent
      - no living spec capability
    best_for: existing Cursor users, lightweight convention enforcement
    pricing: Free-200/month

  Augment Code:
    type: commercial platform
    workflow: context-engine-first
    features:
      - Context Engine (400,000+ file semantic understanding)
      - BYOA model
      - event-driven triggers (GitHub, Linear, Slack, PagerDuty)
      - Organization Knowledge layer
    limitations:
      - no free tier
      - context layer, not spec authoring
      - vendor-reported benchmarks
    best_for: enterprise multi-service, context drift problems
    pricing: $100/month (Business), custom (Enterprise)

  Tessl:
    type: commercial platform
    workflow: spec-as-source
    features:
      - tiles installed in .tessl/ directory
      - Spec Registry (10,000+ library specs)
      - // GENERATED FROM SPEC - DO NOT EDIT
      - MCP-compatible
    limitations:
      - regeneration engine non-deterministic
      - JavaScript-only currently
      - closed beta
    best_for: spec-as-source bet tracking
    pricing: Closed beta

ComparisonDimensions:
  Enforcement:
    GitHub Spec Kit: None (convention)
    Kiro: Review-gated
    OpenSpec: Optional
    BMAD: Process-gated (handoffs)
    CodeMySpec: Mandatory BDD gate + live QA
    Tessl: n/a (pre-product)
    Augment: none
    Cursor: none

  Verification:
    GitHub Spec Kit: None
    Kiro: Agent Hooks (tests)
    OpenSpec: None
    BMAD: None built-in
    CodeMySpec: Live browser QA + tests
    Tessl: Spec Registry
    Augment: None
    Cursor: None

  Portability:
    High: GitHub Spec Kit, OpenSpec, BMAD-METHOD, Cursor, GSD
    Low: Kiro (IDE), Augment (platform), Tessl (platform)

  Spec Lifecycle:
    Living: Augment Cosmos, OpenSpec (semi-living delta markers)
    Static: Kiro, GitHub Spec Kit, BMAD-METHOD, Cursor, Tessl

KeyInsight:
  "Most SDD tools generate a spec and hand off to a separate agent.
   The harder, more useful problem is making the spec govern the code
   and verifying the result, end to end."

State: +
